<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Ajayi Crowther</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1809–1891</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Ajayi Crowther was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> linguist and clergyman who was freed from a slave ship as a boy and educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>. He worked as a missionary at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abeokuta">Abeokuta</a>, translated the Bible into Yoruba and led the Church Missionary Society's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> mission, founding a station at Onitsha in 1857. In 1864 he became the first African Anglican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-ajayi-crowther.jpg" alt="Samuel Ajayi Crowther" width="320" height="565" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bishop_Samuel_Ajayi_Crowther_1867.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
